## 3. Rules

1. **No shadow AI:** use only the declared, organisation-approved tools of section 2, with organisation accounts; never use an AI tool on a personal account for {{org_name}} development or QMS work. Label every AI-assisted change (section 4). Report any AI tool, extension, bot or agent you find that is not in section 2 (section 6); it needs registration and validation first. AI use the organisation does not know about cannot be validated, controlled or defended in an audit.
2. **Accounts:** only the organisation account or workspace, through single sign-on. No foreign or shared accounts or tokens. Agent and CI tokens are issued and recorded by the tool owner.
3. **Data:** never put secrets, patient or health data or personal data into prompts, context or instruction files, or repositories the tool can read, unless the contract and [fill in: data protection assessment] cover it. Use synthetic data for tests.
4. **Untrusted content:** issues, PR comments, web pages and MCP results can carry instructions aimed at the agent (prompt injection). Do not combine such content with write access to protected systems.
5. **You own every line you merge.** As author: keep changes reviewable; read and understand every changed line; never let the AI delete tests, weaken assertions or change expected values to make CI pass; check that every new dependency exists and is approved (AI tools invent package names).
6. **As reviewer:** review the diff, not the AI's summary; approve only what you understood; check tests, dependencies, secrets and changes to CI, gate, tool configuration or instruction files (these need code-owner review). AI review comments are hints, and their absence proves nothing.
7. **Separation of duties:** never approve your own PR or a PR whose agent you launched. Where the agent opens PRs under its own identity, the code host counts your approval, so this rule is yours to keep; such PRs need 2 approvals or code-owner approval.
8. **Models:** only models from the approved list, and a newly released model only after its admission record (MA-01) exists. Report unexpected model switches to the tool owner.
9. **Configuration:** managed or admin configuration changes only by the {{role_tool_owner}}, through change control. Instruction files guide the AI; they do not enforce anything.
10. **Prohibited:** letting the AI approve, merge, sign, close or release anything; recording an approval for someone else; merging without approvals, pushing directly to a protected branch or bypassing gates outside [fill in: emergency procedure]; permission-bypass or unsandboxed modes; MCP servers, plugins or extensions not on the allow-list; agent write access to QMS systems outside the code-host gate (for example through MCP) unless validated for it; asking the AI to execute, conclude or approve the validation of an AI tool.
